(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has had three episodes of pericarditis in 18 months, each treated in primary care with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Cardiology","Gastroenterology","Infectious disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Referral to cardiology is advised for recurrences (recurrent pericarditis), for no response to conservative management and for the development of constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with a prosthetic mitral valve is having a gum biopsy. She is not allergic to penicillin, but she cannot swallow pills after a recent stroke. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Injected clindamycin","Ampicillin by injection","No prophylaxis","Oral amoxicillin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patient without penicillin allergy who cannot take oral medication receives ampicillin intramuscularly or intravenously (or cefazolin or ceftriaxone) 30-60 minutes before. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man in cardiac arrest is intubated by the team. Compressions continue. How should breaths now be given?","choices":["Compressions without breaths","One breath every 6 seconds","One breath every 2 seconds","Two breaths per 30 compressions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Once intubated, cardiopulmonary resuscitation continues as continuous chest compressions with 1 breath every 6 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a moderate pericardial effusion was stable yesterday. Today her blood pressure is 96/64, her jugular venous pressure is elevated, and she has new sinus tachycardia at 128. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Immediate pericardiocentesis","Increase her loop diuretic","Rate control with a beta blocker","Repeat echocardiogram next week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In tamponade, sinus tachycardia may indicate significant hemodynamic compromise and is an indication for immediate pericardiocentesis, with fluid support until it is done. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why is renal function checked in peripheral artery disease?","choices":["Risk of renal insufficiency","To measure homocysteine","Risk of kidney stones","To grade the claudication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients with peripheral artery disease are at risk for renal insufficiency, so renal function joins the electrocardiogram, blood count, lipid profile, metabolic panel and hemoglobin A1c. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 4-month-old with tachycardia, poor feeding, respiratory distress and hypotension is being worked up for sepsis. A new murmur is heard. Why must endocarditis be considered even though the presentation looks like sepsis?","choices":["Infants have marked joint pain","Infants rarely develop sepsis","Infants show classic Osler nodes","Infants present nonspecifically"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In neonates and infants endocarditis presents nonspecifically and is often indistinguishable from sepsis or heart failure: feeding difficulty, respiratory distress, tachycardia, hypotension and a new or changing murmur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has three months of progressive hoarseness. A chest radiograph shows widening of the mediastinal shadow. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Descending aortic aneurysm","Ascending aortic aneurysm","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Aortic arch aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic aneurysm symptoms depend on location: an arch aneurysm causes hoarseness by involving the recurrent laryngeal nerve, and the chest film shows a widened mediastinum. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has new pauses and slow rates on an ambulatory monitor. He started verapamil and digoxin last month for palpitations. He feels mildly lightheaded but is otherwise well. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Atropine tablets","Permanent pacemaker now","Stop the rate-slowing drugs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Medications (beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin) are listed causes of sick sinus syndrome, and management starts by treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"After a positive D-dimer in the low likelihood Wells category, which test comes next?","choices":["A repeat D-dimer","Stopping the work-up","Contrast venography","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A positive D-dimer is nonspecific, so it is followed by ultrasound of the leg; a positive ultrasound gives the diagnosis and a negative one rules it out. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman treated for breast cancer 2 years ago has 3 weeks of fatigue and exertional dyspnea. Her blood pressure is 128/80 without hypotension. The chest radiograph shows an enlarged, water-bottle (globular) cardiac silhouette with clear lung fields. Which test is required to confirm a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Coronary angiography","Repeat chest radiograph","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram","Transthoracic echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clinical evaluation, electrocardiogram and chest radiograph may suggest a pericardial effusion, but an echocardiogram is required to confirm it and to assess its hemodynamic significance. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man ran out of his metoprolol four days ago. He feels his heart racing, and his pulse is 118 and regular, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. He has no fever and is not bleeding. Which is the most likely cause of his tachycardia?","choices":["Beta blocker withdrawal","Hypothyroidism","Raised intracranial pressure","Increased vagal tone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt withdrawal of a beta blocker (or a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker) is a listed cause of sinus tachycardia, which fits stopping metoprolol four days ago. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman who received pelvic radiation for cervical cancer four years ago has two weeks of left calf swelling measuring 4 cm more than the right, pitting edema confined to that leg, and tenderness along the deep veins. Her cancer is in remission and untreated. Her clinician judges chronic lymphedema at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis. Which test comes next by the Wells criteria?","choices":["D-dimer blood test","Venous duplex ultrasound","Contrast ascending venography","No testing is required"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three positive items (calf swelling, pitting edema, deep venous tenderness) give 3 points, and the likelier alternative diagnosis subtracts 2, leaving 1, a moderate likelihood. Moderate scores of 1 to 2 need a D-dimer. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with cardiomyopathy has become lightheaded on standing since his medications were increased at his last visit. He is frail and has lost weight. Which parts of his drug therapy call for particular caution at his age?","choices":["Diuretics and vasodilators","Home-based rehabilitation","Sodium restriction advice","Implanted device checks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age-specific strategies for elderly patients with cardiomyopathy call for cautious use of diuretics and vasodilators, with therapy tailored to comorbidity and frailty and a focus on symptom control. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia with some rates approaching 300 appears in someone with a known accessory pathway. Which intravenous drug is used?","choices":["Procainamide","Magnesium","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia approaching 300 with an accessory pathway is atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White (ventricular tachycardia would be regular at 150 to 200), treated with intravenous procainamide, never adenosine. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with chest pain and breathlessness after a recent viral illness is being evaluated for myocarditis. Her electrocardiogram shows sinus tachycardia. Which laboratory result would indicate cardiac myonecrosis?","choices":["A low white cell count","A low natriuretic peptide","Elevated serum troponin","Normal sedimentation rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serum troponin is the indicator of cardiac myonecrosis in myocarditis. The rest of the work-up adds leukocytosis on the blood count, a possibly elevated sedimentation rate and electrocardiogram changes. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has visible, dilated, tortuous veins of the thigh and calf that are tender to palpation, with aching heaviness after prolonged standing. Which is the most common predisposing factor for her condition?","choices":["Recent pregnancy","Family history of varicose veins","Prolonged standing at work","Regular heavy lifting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The most common predisposing factor for varicose veins is family history, so ask whether relatives have them, and how old they were when they developed. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a positive D-dimer during evaluation of calf pain two weeks after a bicycle crash with extensive bruising. He asks why his clinician says the result cannot confirm a clot. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It rises only with pulmonary embolism","It reflects fibrinolysis from many causes","It rises only with thigh clots","It is specific but not sensitive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer elevation implies increased fibrinolysis, but it is sensitive and not specific. Bruising, surgery, cancer and many other conditions raise it, so a positive result needs imaging while a negative one helps rule out a clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with varicose veins has a small, tender, red cord along the great saphenous vein of the calf. Venous duplex ultrasound confirms a short segment of superficial clot. She has no other risk factors for thrombosis. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Inferior vena cava filter placement","Warm compresses and ibuprofen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Typical treatment is warm compresses, compression, elevation of the limb and an anti-inflammatory drug such as ibuprofen or diclofenac for pain and inflammation. Anticoagulation is reserved for higher risk. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy also has enlarged hilar lymph nodes and skin nodules, and an infiltrative process such as sarcoidosis is suspected. Coronary angiography showed no disease. Which imaging test best identifies an inflammatory or infiltrative cause?","choices":["Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium","A repeat resting electrocardiogram in six months","Cardiac catheterization with pressure tracings","Ambulatory rhythm monitoring for forty-eight hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium contrast helps identify inflammatory, ischemic or infiltrative processes such as sarcoidosis or hemochromatosis in dilated cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman recovering well from her first episode of acute viral pericarditis asks how likely it is to come back. Which estimate is most accurate?","choices":["Under 1%","About 15 to 30%","About 60%","Over 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis affects about 15 to 30% of patients after acute pericarditis, which is why completing the full course and recognizing symptoms early matter. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has a preoperative electrocardiogram before cataract surgery. He feels well. His heart rate is 70, every P wave is followed by a narrow QRS complex, and the PR interval is constant at 0.26 seconds on every beat. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mobitz type I block","Mobitz type II block","First-degree block","Complete heart block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A constant PR interval longer than 0.20 seconds with every P wave conducted to a narrow QRS is first-degree atrioventricular block, most often from age-related degeneration of the conduction system. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Uremic pericarditis is typically associated with a blood urea nitrogen above which level?","choices":["10 mg/dL","20 mg/dL","60 mg/dL","200 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uremic pericarditis is linked to a blood urea nitrogen above 60 mg/dL; there will be laboratory evidence and a history of kidney disease, and it is treated with dialysis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a rhythm strip in which a normal beat is followed by an early beat with an abnormal P wave and a narrow QRS, repeating so that every other beat is early (bigeminy). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Flutter with 2:1 block","Ventricular bigeminy","Junctional rhythm","Atrial bigeminy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early beats with an abnormal P wave and narrow QRS are premature atrial contractions (common in chronic lung disease), and an ectopic beat every other beat is bigeminy. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man who had a large anterior myocardial infarction three weeks ago develops sudden pain and coldness of his right leg with absent foot pulses. Which cardiac finding is the most likely embolic source?","choices":["A pericardial effusion","An atrial septal scar","Left ventricular thrombus","A calcified mitral leaflet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thromboemboli are the most common emboli, and the heart is the source in 80 to 90% of cases; a left ventricular thrombus after myocardial infarction is a named example, as is atrial fibrillation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man has mild breathlessness and fatigue two weeks after a viral upper respiratory infection. Troponin is mildly raised, echocardiogram shows mildly reduced function, and he is hemodynamically stable. Which is the mainstay of his treatment?","choices":["Transplant listing","Supportive care","Immediate biopsy","Septal ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of myocarditis is directed toward the clinical scenario, and supportive care is the mainstay, with vasodilators, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, diuretics, antiarrhythmics or anticoagulation as needed. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How are antibiotics for pediatric infective endocarditis given?","choices":["After surgery, not before","Intramuscularly and fixed-dose","Intravenously and weight-based","Orally for one week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pediatric therapy is prolonged, bactericidal, parenteral (intravenous, not intramuscular), pathogen-directed and weight-based, with the same empiric therapy as adults. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with suspected endocarditis has a urinalysis sent as part of her workup. Which urinalysis result is most consistent with endocarditis?","choices":["Blood, protein and white cells","Nitrites alone, no cells","Glucose and ketones","Bilirubin and urobilinogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Urinalysis in endocarditis can show hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria, reflecting renal involvement such as glomerulonephritis or emboli. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has new atrial fibrillation at 146 with palpitations. She is alert, her blood pressure is 132/84, and she has no chest pain or breathlessness. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous rate control","Intravenous atropine","Immediate ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response gets intravenous rate control; rate control is the first goal. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl nearly fainted twice while standing in the lunch line; both times she felt better once she lay down. She plays competitive soccer. A systolic murmur is heard at the left sternal border that becomes louder with the Valsalva maneuver. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Recheck the murmur in a year","Clear her to play next week","Urgent echocardiography","Reassure; common in teenagers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Presyncope on standing relieved by lying down, like syncope, identifies patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy at high risk of sudden death, so the work-up is urgent, and echocardiography is the typical test of choice. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has a stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 150 in which P waves cannot be clearly seen. Adenosine is given. Besides possibly ending the rhythm, what can it reveal?","choices":["A bundle branch block","Hidden atrial activity","ST elevation from infarction","A prolonged QT interval"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine may convert a supraventricular tachycardia to normal sinus rhythm, or at least slow the rate so you can see whether P waves are there. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and malaise that began a week after a dental extraction. A new murmur of mitral regurgitation is heard. Three sets of blood cultures are drawn. Which organism are they most likely to grow?","choices":["Enterococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Candida species","Viridans streptococci"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The oral cavity is the source of viridans streptococci, the classic cause of subacute endocarditis after dental procedures and the organism dental prophylaxis targets. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy returns with recurring ankle edema and increasing abdominal girth. She admits to eating canned soups and fast food most days. Which dietary counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Restrict sodium to under two grams a day","No diet change is needed with drugs","Raise salt intake to protect pressure","Drink extra fluids with every meal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for dilated cardiomyopathy includes sodium restriction to less than two grams a day, along with the medication regimen, follow-up and removing any offending source. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of viridans streptococcal infective endocarditis?","choices":["Worse than Staphylococcus aureus","Poor even with prompt treatment","Valve replacement in most","Relatively good if treated early"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococcal endocarditis has a relatively good prognosis if antibiotic treatment is started before complications occur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman taking a loop diuretic for heart failure has new palpitations felt in her neck. Telemetry shows frequent premature ventricular contractions. Which laboratory values should be checked first?","choices":["Glucose and lactate","Troponin and lipase","Potassium and magnesium","Thyroid hormone and cortisol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Electrolyte imbalances, especially hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia (with hypercalcemia), are main causes of premature ventricular contractions, and a loop diuretic can lower both. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does the transthoracic echocardiogram show in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Septal wall of 1.5 cm or more","Big atria, stiff normal ventricles","Four dilated, thin-walled chambers","Mid and apical wall hypokinesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The transthoracic echocardiogram shows wall-motion abnormality, hypokinesis or akinesis of the midsegment and apical segment of the left ventricle. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with acute pericarditis says the pain keeps him awake because it is worse when he lies down. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Sleep prone to splint the chest","Sleep flat to rest the heart","Keep the head of the bed elevated","Take an extra dose at bedtime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial pain is worse supine and better leaning forward, so keeping the head of the bed elevated is advised, along with completing the full treatment course. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Idarucizumab is the reversal agent for which anticoagulant?","choices":["Dabigatran","Apixaban","Warfarin","Unfractionated heparin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Idarucizumab reverses dabigatran in life-threatening or intracranial hemorrhage; each reversal agent is specific to its anticoagulant. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had symmetric pitting swelling of both ankles for three weeks. He started amlodipine for hypertension one month ago. There is no warmth, redness, tenderness or skin discoloration, and he has no history of heart, kidney or liver disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic venous insufficiency","Bilateral deep vein thrombosis","Drug-induced edema","Lymphedema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Swelling that is often bilateral, without signs of inflammation, is a common adverse effect of some drugs, including dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension has sudden, severe, ripping chest pain. His electrocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Ankle-brachial index measurement","Abdominal plain radiograph","Computed tomography, chest and abdomen","Reassure him; the tracing is normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram may be normal in aortic dissection, so a normal tracing does not exclude it; computed tomography of the chest and abdomen delineates the extent of the dissection. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with a pacemaker implanted two weeks ago has three days of swelling and aching of the left arm. The pacemaker lead has injured the wall of the subclavian vein. Which component of the Virchow triad does this injury represent?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Hypercoagulable state","Impaired lymphatic drainage","Venous stasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Damage to the vessel lining from an indwelling device is endothelial trauma, one leg of the Virchow triad. Pacemakers, implanted defibrillators and central venous catheters are the most common causes of upper-extremity deep vein thrombosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has months of progressive breathlessness, abdominal swelling and bilateral pedal edema. He prefers to sit upright. Jugular venous pressure is raised and he has a Kussmaul sign (jugular venous pressure rises with inspiration), hepatomegaly and ascites. His electrocardiogram shows low voltage (small complexes) in all leads, and echocardiogram shows normal-sized ventricles, marked biatrial enlargement and a preserved ejection fraction. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stiff ventricles with a near-normal cavity, preserved ejection fraction and big atria, presenting late with right-sided failure, are restrictive cardiomyopathy; low voltage points to amyloidosis, the most common cause in the United States. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What makes up atheroemboli?","choices":["Fat droplets from bone marrow","Clot formed in the left atrium","Fibrin, platelets and cholesterol","Air drawn into a vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atheroembolism is multiple small deposits of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris embolizing from proximal atherosclerotic lesions. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has two weeks of exertional breathlessness and chest pressure. Her electrocardiogram shows a left bundle branch block; a tracing from last year was normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure: a normal variant","Evaluate for heart disease","Place a pacemaker now","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A new left bundle branch block should always be considered a sign of pathology, so it is worked up for the underlying cardiac disease or injury rather than dismissed. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Besides shocking, what can newer implantable cardioverter defibrillators also do?","choices":["Ablate scar tissue","Deliver antiarrhythmics","Dissolve coronary clots","Act as pacemakers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Newer implantable defibrillators can also act as pacemakers, in addition to sensing fatal arrhythmias and delivering a shock. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic kidney disease has native valve endocarditis from penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci. The team is choosing his antibiotic plan. Which choice should be avoided?","choices":["Ceftriaxone for the full course","The 2-week gentamicin regimen","Penicillin G for the full course","Monitoring renal function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The 2-week regimen with gentamicin is reserved for uncomplicated endocarditis with a rapid response and no chronic kidney disease; gentamicin is nephrotoxic, so he needs a standard course without it. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a 4.8 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm has a history of severe reaction to contrast dye. She is stable, and the extent of the aneurysm must be defined. Which imaging is most appropriate?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Abdominal X-ray","Conventional angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance imaging is comparable to computed tomography without exposure to dye or ionizing radiation, which makes it useful in stable patients with a dye allergy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"When right bundle branch block causes symptoms, what do they usually point to?","choices":["Isolated electrolyte loss","A harmless normal variant","An accessory pathway","Severe underlying heart disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Right bundle branch block is usually asymptomatic; patients who develop symptoms usually have severe underlying cardiac disease, with heart failure-like symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man underwent cardiac catheterization through the right femoral artery and vein ten days ago. He now has a continuous machine-like murmur over the puncture site with a palpable thrill (a vibration felt over the skin), and his right leg is swollen with prominent superficial veins. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Iatrogenic arteriovenous fistula","Deep vein thrombosis of the leg","Femoral pseudoaneurysm","Superficial thrombophlebitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Femoral puncture for percutaneous access can connect the artery and vein, and the bruit and thrill with venous congestion of the leg follow. Lower extremity fistulas are most commonly iatrogenic from groin access. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man had his pacemaker generator changed 3 weeks ago. He now has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), and imaging shows a vegetation on a pacemaker lead. Which organisms cause the largest share of this type of endocarditis?","choices":["Enterococci","Viridans streptococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Coagulase-negative staphylococci"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Device endocarditis is caused mostly by coagulase-negative staphylococci (about 41%) and Staphylococcus aureus (about 36%), and it can appear within weeks of implantation or a generator change. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with known atrioventricular nodal reentry has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 178. She is stable. Which treatment almost always terminates it?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Intravenous procainamide","Vagal maneuvers or adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular nodal reentry almost always terminates with vagal maneuvers or adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is rarely required. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man received chest radiation for lymphoma 20 years ago. He has 8 months of exertional dyspnea, fatigue, leg edema and ascites. His jugular venous pressure is elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (it rises on inspiration instead of falling), and a pericardial knock (an early diastolic sound, slightly earlier than a third heart sound) is heard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Constrictive pericarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Acute pericarditis","Infective endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluid overload with low output, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock after prior radiation are the picture of constrictive pericarditis, scarring that limits filling. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has months of fatigue and two near-faints. Repeated electrocardiograms show sinus bradycardia at 42, and her symptoms match the slow rate. She takes no rate-slowing drugs and her thyroid tests are normal. Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Synchronized cardioversion","Atropine as needed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic, symptomatic sinus bradycardia with no reversible cause, as here in an older adult, is managed with a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with crushing chest pain develops a wide QRS tachycardia whose complexes vary in shape from beat to beat; his QT before the episode was normal. He is still talking and has a pulse. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Observation on the monitor","Intravenous amiodarone alone","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, only a short-lived transitional state, so it is defibrillated even when the patient has a pulse and looks well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has ischemic rest pain and a nonhealing toe ulcer, with an ankle-brachial index of 0.3. Angiography shows a long femoral-popliteal occlusion. Which treatment is principally used for her degree of disease?","choices":["Intravenous heparin alone","Pentoxifylline alone","Supervised exercise alone","Bypass graft surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A bypass graft, using a saphenous vein or synthetic graft around the diseased segment, is principally used to treat severe critical leg ischemia and is highly effective. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with carotid artery disease on duplex smokes a pack a day and asks whether quitting still matters now that he takes aspirin. Which counseling point is accurate?","choices":["Smoking nearly doubles stroke risk","Smoking harms only the leg arteries","Quitting only matters before surgery","Aspirin cancels the smoking risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is part of medical management of carotid disease because smoking nearly doubles the risk of stroke; it sits alongside antiplatelet therapy, comorbidity control, diet, exercise and weight reduction. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman on telemetry has torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). The first-line intravenous push broke the first run, but short runs keep recurring. She is alert with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Magnesium infusion","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous amiodarone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Torsades in a conscious patient is broken with intravenous magnesium and then treated with more magnesium as an infusion: magnesium, and more magnesium. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man who injects drugs has finished a course of intravenous antibiotics for tricuspid valve endocarditis and is preparing for discharge. He says he wants help to stay safe. Which consultation should be arranged?","choices":["Nephrology","Ophthalmology","Addiction medicine","Dermatology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Addiction medicine is consulted for harm reduction in people who inject drugs, part of the multidisciplinary care with infectious disease and cardiothoracic surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with right lower lobe pneumonia has sharp chest pain. A rubbing sound is heard over the lower chest that is present only while she breathes in and vanishes when she holds her breath. Which is the most likely source of the sound?","choices":["The mitral valve","The pericardium","The pleura","The chest wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pleural rub is tied to respiration, heard in the inspiratory phase, and disappears with breath-holding; a pericardial rub persists, and even intensifies, while the breath is held. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How does older age affect surgical intervention for pericardial disease?","choices":["Higher morbidity and mortality","Lower risk than in younger adults","No change in surgical risk","Surgery is ruled out entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Older adults have higher morbidity and mortality with surgical interventions, including pericardiocentesis and pericardiectomy, and often need rehabilitation afterward. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman is brought in after fainting at home. She is confused, her heart rate is 34 and her blood pressure is 78/40. The electrocardiogram shows regular P waves, a constant PR interval in the conducted beats and many P waves with no QRS after them. Medications given so far have not raised her rate. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Transcutaneous pacing","Defibrillation","Observation on a monitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When medications are not used or do not work in unstable Mobitz type II block, the emergent measure is transcutaneous pacing, which bridges her to a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"}]};
